    "result_text": "Full suite is green \u2014 **21 tests, 0 failures**. The change is complete.\n\n## What I did\n\nAddress normalization is now applied whenever an owner is created, following the project's existing normalize-in-place pattern (mirroring `NormalizeOwnerEmail` / `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`).\n\n1. **New step `NormalizeOwnerAddress`** (`rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerAddress.java`) \u2014 reads the published body (`@Val OwnerFieldsDto`) and rewrites `address` in place: trim, collapse internal whitespace to a single space, upper-case, then expand whole-token abbreviations (`ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD`, `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE`). Exposes a static `normalize(String)` so the rule lives in one place. Because `@Val` yields the stored object (not a copy), the normalized value flows to every downstream step and is what gets stored and returned.\n\n2. **Wired into the POST pipeline** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted `normalizeAddress` right after `require` and before the telephone/household/build steps (under `govern: [ transaction ]`), so all later comparisons and the persisted entity see the normalized address.\n\n3. **Required-field check updated** (`RequireOwnerFields.java`) \u2014 the `address` presence check now runs against `NormalizeOwnerAddress.normalize(...)`, so an address that is blank after normalization is rejected with a 400.\n\n4. **Household comparisons** (`RequireUniqueOwnerHousehold`, `AssignHouseholdId`) needed no change: since the address is fully normalized in place before those steps run \u2014 and existing stored owners were created through the same pipeline \u2014 both sides of every duplicate-detection and household-id comparison are the normalized form.\n\nThis satisfies the new `Cp12` tests (normalization + blank rejection) and keeps `Cp10` (duplicate detection) and `Cp11` (shared household id) passing, along with the rest of the suite. Only the create path is affected, as the spec specifies.",
